package preamble

// Keep this in sync with the builtins in the common model
var builtinDescriptions = map[string]string{

	// GSL operations

	"acosh":     "Takes one argument, e, and returns the inverse hyperbolic cosine of e.",
	"asinh":     "Takes one argument, e, and returns the inverse hyperbolic sine of e.",
	"atanh":     "Takes one argument, e, and returns the inverse hyperbolic tangent of e.",
	"clamp":     "Takes three arguments, e1, e2, and e3, and returns e1 clamped to the range [e2, e3].",
	"cosh":      "Takes one argument, e, and returns the hyperbolic cosine of e.",
	"cross":     "Takes two arguments, e1 and e2, and returns the cross product of e1 and e2.",
	"degrees":   "Takes one argument, e, and converts radians to degrees.",
	"distance":  "Takes two arguments, e1 and e2, and returns the distance between e1 and e2.",
	"dot":       "Takes two arguments, e1 and e2, and returns the dot product of e1 and e2.",
	"erfinv":    "Takes one argument, e, a constant in (-1, 1), and returns the inverse error function of e. It is evaluated at compile time only: sqrt(2) * erfinv(2 * q - 1) is the standard-normal quantile, the level that leaves fraction q of a unit-variance Gaussian field below it.",
	"exp2":      "Takes one argument, e, and returns 2 raised to the power of e.",
	"fract":     "Takes one argument, e, and returns the fractional part of e.",
	"length":    "Takes one argument, v, and returns the length of vector v.",
	"normalize": "Takes one argument, v, and returns a vector in the same direction as v but with length 1.",
	"radians":   "Takes one argument, e, and converts degrees to radians.",
	"sign":      "Takes one argument, e, and returns the sign of e.",
	"sinh":      "Takes one argument, e, and returns the hyperbolic sine of e.",
	"tanh":      "Takes one argument, e, and returns the hyperbolic tangent of e.",
	"trunc":     "Takes one argument, e, and returns the integer part of e.",

	// Noise

	"warpField":         "Simulated displacement at a position: warpField(p) returns the vec3 offset a simulation predicts at world point p, in mm, or [0,0,0] outside the simulated region. Sampled from a baked field, so it is a runtime lookup rather than a constant. Its purpose is to deform geometry through remap: remap(part, p + warpField(p)) shows the distorted shape, and negating it — remap(part, p - warpField(p)) — pre-distorts a shape so the result comes out closer to nominal. The field carries the *residual* warp, with uniform scaling already removed, because the shape is separately scaled by its own factor; compensating with this on top of that scale corrects shape without double-counting scaling. Requires a simulation to have been run for the document.",
	"pixelSize":         "The size of one screen pixel in world units (mm) at the point being shaded, for fading out detail too fine to show: a texture can blend a feature toward its average color once pixelSize() approaches the feature's size, instead of letting it shimmer. Takes no arguments. It is 0 wherever nothing is being drawn to the screen (meshing, export, the CPU renderer, the ray march itself), so code that fades by it gets full detail there. Meant for .color and .material; geometry should not depend on it.",
	"displacementDrawn": "How much of a material's displacement (material.withDisplacement) is drawn at the point being shaded, from 0 to 1: 1 where the renderer moves the surface by the full height, falling to 0 as the Detail slider takes the displacement away from a part that is small on screen. Takes no arguments. It is 1 in the CPU renderer, which draws every displacement in full, and 0 wherever displacement is not drawn (meshing, export, the simple tier), so a color can stand in for a displacement: mix the displaced look's plain color toward a painted version of the relief by 1 - displacementDrawn(), and the relief shows as paint wherever it is not geometry. Meant for .color; geometry should not depend on it.",

	"worleyF1":         "Low-level procedural-noise primitive: takes a 3-component coordinate on the unit cellular (Worley) lattice and returns the distance to the nearest feature point, where each lattice cell holds one point jittered inside it. Scans the 3x3x3 neighbourhood. Forms: worleyF1(q) and worleyF1(q, octaves), where octaves is a literal 1 or 2 selecting the feature-point hash's tile lookups (see noiseHash3). Exactly 1-Lipschitz, so it is safe to sphere-trace and to offset. Most users call the noise library (noise.cellular) rather than this directly; the library scales it from cell units to world units.",
	"worleyF2":         "Low-level procedural-noise primitive: the distance from a 3-component coordinate on the unit cellular (Worley) lattice to the SECOND-nearest feature point. Same lattice, forms and octaves argument as worleyF1, and also exactly 1-Lipschitz (the k-th smallest of 1-Lipschitz functions is 1-Lipschitz), so it is equally safe to sphere-trace. worleyF2 - worleyF1 is the Voronoi-edge field, ~0 along cell walls. Most users call noise.cellularEdges or noise.cellularF1F2 instead.",
	"worleyFeature":    "Low-level procedural-noise primitive: the feature point worleyF1 measures to — the nearest lattice feature point to a 3-component coordinate q, as a vec3 in the same cell units. Piecewise constant: it changes only when q crosses a Voronoi wall. Carries the derivative of worleyF1 (d|v| = dot(v, v')/|v| with v = q - worleyFeature(q)), which is the true analytic gradient, a unit vector away from that point. Same forms and octaves argument as worleyF1.",
	"worleyFarFeature": "Low-level procedural-noise primitive: the feature point worleyF2 measures to — the SECOND-nearest lattice feature point to q, as a vec3 in cell units. One scan knows both closest points, so it knows both gradients: this carries worleyF2's derivative exactly as worleyFeature carries worleyF1's, with no extra hash. Piecewise constant. Same forms and octaves argument as worleyF1.",
	"worleyNearCell":   "Low-level procedural-noise primitive: the integer lattice cell whose feature point is nearest a 3-component coordinate q, as a vec3 in cell units. Constant over a whole Voronoi cell, so hashing it gives one random value per cell — one colour per crystal, pebble or grain. Piecewise constant: it changes only as q crosses a cell wall. Same forms and octaves argument as worleyF1. Most users call noise.cellularCell instead.",
	"worleyFarCell":    "Low-level procedural-noise primitive: the integer lattice cell owning the SECOND-nearest feature point to q, as a vec3 in cell units. Together with worleyNearCell it names the wall between two cells — the unordered pair is the same on both sides of that wall, so a hash of it decides the wall as a whole, which is how veins and crazing follow some walls and not others. Same forms and octaves argument as worleyF1. Most users call noise.cellularPair instead.",

	"gaborSpinodoid":         "Low-level spinodoid primitive: the lattice Gabor spinodoid at a 3-component point q, hodge's spinodoid_gabor model with its kernels on a regular lattice and their envelopes tapered to zero at the cutoff. Every backend evaluates this field: preview, meshing, export and the oracle. A tape carries its arguments, so a reader that has to agree with hodge can rebuild hodge's own field, a different realization that measures the same. Form: gaborSpinodoid(q, period, theta, poles, packetWidth, impulses, pool, seed, bias, localNormalize, scale, orientation). theta lists the cone half-angles in radians, one per pole; poles is one axis per angle (a list of vec3). period is the wavelength; packetWidth the envelope width in wavelengths (hodge: 3); impulses the kernels per cell (hodge: 8); pool the direction-pool size (hodge: 4096); seed the seed; bias the level the level set sits at, in units of the field's standard deviation like a TPMS level t (0 is the balanced interface, positive grows the solid phase; lattice/spinodoid's ...BySolidFraction forms take a solid fraction instead); localNormalize 1 divides by the local wavenumber, 0 by the base one. All of these must be constants: they key a kernel table baked at compile time. scale (a wavelength multiplier, > 0) and orientation (a 3x3 rotation applied to each wave direction) are fields of p, sampled once at every kernel centre when the table is baked. Returns a unit-RMS-gradient pseudo-distance, negative in the solid phase. Graded fields sample their drivers over the imported geometry bounds, or an explicit scene size, without repeating them. Stationary fields retain a 64-site periodic kernel table. Headerless analytical fields and fields wrapped in an outer coordinate remap retain the legacy periodic table. A direct query-point transform is bounded in query space. Most users call lattice/spinodoid instead.",
	"gaborSpinodoidGradient": "Low-level spinodoid primitive: the analytic gradient of gaborSpinodoid at q, as a vec3, from the same baked kernel table and with the same twelve arguments. It carries gaborSpinodoid's derivative.",

	"fastGaborSpinodoid":         "Low-level spinodoid primitive: the lattice Gabor spinodoid, with exactly the twelve arguments of gaborSpinodoid. It is the same field as gaborSpinodoid, kept so documents that name it still build. Its kernel grid repeats every 64 sites. Most users call lattice/spinodoid instead.",
	"fastGaborSpinodoidGradient": "Low-level spinodoid primitive: the analytic gradient of fastGaborSpinodoid at q, as a vec3, from the same baked table and with the same twelve arguments. It carries fastGaborSpinodoid's derivative.",

	"noiseHash3": "Low-level procedural-noise primitive: takes a 3-component cell coordinate and returns a pseudo-random vec3 in [0,1)^3 for that cell, by sampling a baked, tileable noise tile. Deterministic and identical across the WGSL and Fidget backends. Forms: noiseHash3(cell) takes two lookups — a nearest lookup plus one supercell fold — and repeats every 4096 cells; noiseHash3(cell, octaves) takes the octave count as a literal 1 or 2, where 1 is the bare nearest lookup, half the cost (one texture fetch instead of two) but repeating every 64 cells. Prefer 1 whenever 64 cells of pattern outruns the part: a 27-neighbour cellular scan pays this cost 27 times per evaluation. Used by the noise library to seed cellular-noise feature points and gradient-noise gradients; most users call the noise library (e.g. noise.cellular) rather than this directly.",

	// Mesh operations

	"uiPoint":        "Marks a 3-component vector as editable by a 3DOF scene manipulator. It returns the vector unchanged for code generation.",
	"uiTransform":    "Marks a homogeneous 4x4 matrix as editable by a 6DOF scene manipulator. It returns the matrix unchanged for code generation.",
	"uiColor":        "Marks a 3-component sRGB colour as editable by a colour control. It returns the vector unchanged for code generation.",
	"uiDirection":    "Marks a 3-component direction as editable by an arrow manipulator in the scene: uiDirection(dir) draws the arrow from the origin, uiDirection(dir, anchor) from the point anchor, a constant or a uiPoint. It returns the direction unchanged for code generation.",
	"uiSpline":       "Groups an ordered list of uiTransform matrices into an editable spline overlay and returns the list unchanged. Use path = uiSpline([start, bend, end]) with a function that takes path[0], path[1], path[2], .... Dotted lines connect the control handles and a thin black quadratic Bezier path connects their translations, visible through scene geometry. Longer odd lists form chained quadratic sections sharing endpoints.",
	"mesh":           "Places an imported mesh in the scene with a homogeneous 4x4 matrix placement. Forms: mesh(index) places it at the identity; mesh(index, M) applies the 4x4 matrix M (16 column-major values, e.g. from the matrix package); mesh(index, position) translates by the [x, y, z] vector; mesh(index, position, R) applies rotation matrix R with its translation set to position. 'index' is a non-negative integer literal selecting an imported mesh by its session import order (0 is the first mesh imported). Build placements with the matrix package (matrix.translation, matrix.scale, matrix.rotation, and multiplication). Arguments after the index are constant-folded and may use literals, arithmetic, math builtins, and references to top-level constants. mesh() is a scene declaration, not an SDF — it cannot be combined with min/max or used in remap.",
	"importStep":     "Imports a STEP file asset into the workspace. Forms: importStep(path) is the expression form that imports the STEP file at path and returns a unique mesh slot id; importStep(index, path) is the numbered statement form that imports the STEP file at path into the given session index. The index/slot is used by mesh() to place the part, and by bvhSdf() / vdbSdf() to query distances.",
	"importMesh":     "Imports a mesh file asset (STL/OBJ/etc.) into the workspace. Forms: importMesh(path) is the expression form that imports the mesh file at path and returns a unique mesh slot id; importMesh(index, path) is the numbered statement form that imports the mesh file at path into the given session index. The index/slot is used by mesh() to place the part, and by bvhSdf() / vdbSdf() to query distances.",
	"importWarp":     "Imports a solved displacement field into the workspace. Forms: importWarp(path) is the expression form that binds the simulation at path and returns a unique warp slot id; importWarp(index, path) is the numbered statement form that binds it into the given slot. Warp slots are numbered separately from mesh slots. The slot is used by meshWarped() / vdbSdfWarped() to deform an imported mesh by the field.",
	"bvhSdf":         "Returns the signed distance from the implicit position p to an imported mesh, sampled by a BVH-accelerated triangle-set distance oracle. Takes one argument: bvhSdf(index). 'index' is a non-negative integer literal selecting an imported mesh by its session import order. Unlike mesh(), bvhSdf() is a true SDF expression and composes with all GSL operations (offset, min, max, remap, blend, etc.). The WGSL preview samples a      finite-resolution grid (lossy near features); the fidget mesh-out path samples the triangle set exactly.",
	"vdbSdf":         "Returns the signed distance from the implicit position p to an imported mesh, sampled from a pre-baked OpenVDB narrow-band level set. Takes one to three arguments: vdbSdf(index [, voxelDivisor [,           halfBandVoxels]]). 'index' is a non-negative integer literal selecting an imported mesh by its session import order. 'voxelDivisor' (positive integer literal, default 256) is the number of voxels along the mesh's longest  bbox axis — the world-space cell size is bbox-longest / voxelDivisor. Higher values = sharper bake at quadratic memory cost. 'halfBandVoxels' (positive numeric literal, default 3) sets the narrow-band half-width in voxels — wider bands give smoother gradients far from the surface. Composes with all GSL operations (offset, min, max, remap, blend, etc.). The underlying oracle uses openvdb::tools::meshToLevelSet for fast distance queries on   the fidget mesh-out path; prefer this over bvhSdf() when working with large or detailed meshes where exact triangle distance evaluation is too slow.",
	"meshWarped":     "Places an imported mesh deformed by a displacement field. meshWarped(meshIndex, warpIndex, scale [, placement...]) — the trailing placement arguments are exactly mesh()'s, so meshWarped(i, w, 1) is at the identity, meshWarped(i, w, 1, M) applies a 4x4, and meshWarped(i, w, 1, position, R) applies a rotation about a position. 'warpIndex' selects a simulation imported with importWarp, whose slots are numbered separately from mesh slots. 'scale' multiplies the predicted displacement: 1 is the shape predicted after displacement, -1 the shape that lands on nominal after displacement, 0 nominal, and larger magnitudes exaggerate for display. The displacement is baked into a derived mesh asset at compile time, so the result is ordinary geometry — it exports, meshes and re-simulates as the warped shape, unlike remap with warpField(), which the mesher cannot follow. The warp is the residual, with uniform scaling already removed. Like mesh(), this is a scene declaration and not an SDF.",
	"vdbSdfWarped":   "Returns the signed distance to an imported mesh deformed by a displacement field. vdbSdfWarped(meshIndex, warpIndex, scale [, voxelDivisor [, halfBandVoxels]]) — the trailing arguments are exactly vdbSdf()'s. 'warpIndex' selects a simulation imported with importWarp; 'scale' multiplies the predicted displacement (1 as predicted, -1 inverse, 0 nominal). The displacement is baked into a derived mesh asset and the level set built from that, so this is a true SDF that composes with every GSL operation and survives the fidget mesh-out path — which is what distinguishes it from remap(vdbSdf(i), p + warpField(p)), where the warp folds to zero on export.",
	"meshUnion":      "Mesh-CSG operator (export-only) — coerces its operands to meshes and returns their union. Accepts two or more meshes, or a single list of meshes: meshUnion(a, b, c) and meshUnion([a, b, c]) are equivalent and fold left into binary unions. SDF operands get fidget mesh-out before Manifold UNION composes. The result is a mesh, not an SDF: do not pass meshUnion()'s result into min/max/arithmetic. For pure-SDF math, use min and unary - instead. The plan slicer collapses contiguous SDF subtrees so meshUnion(sphere, box) costs the same as min(sphere, box), but every       meshUnion that touches a mesh() literal forces at least one fidget mesh-out per pure-SDF subtree it encloses. The render preview lowers meshUnion to min for visualization; the actual mesh-out + Manifold composition only   runs when the user clicks Save STL.",
	"meshIntersect":  "Mesh-CSG operator (export-only) — coerces its operands to meshes and returns their intersection. Accepts two or more meshes, or a single list of meshes: meshIntersect(a, b, c) and meshIntersect([a, b, c]) are equivalent and fold left into binary intersections. SDF operands get fidget mesh-out before Manifold INTERSECTION composes. The result is a mesh, not an    SDF: do not pass meshIntersect()'s result into min/max/arithmetic. For pure-SDF math, use max instead. The plan slicer collapses contiguous SDF subtrees so meshIntersect(sphere, box) costs the same as max(sphere, box),    but every meshIntersect that touches a mesh() literal forces at least one fidget mesh-out per pure-SDF subtree it encloses. The render preview lowers meshIntersect to max for visualization; the actual mesh-out + Manifold  composition only runs when the user clicks Save STL.",
	"meshDifference": "Mesh-CSG operator (export-only) — takes exactly two operands and returns the first minus the second. Either operand may be a single mesh or a list of meshes. The first operand's meshes are unioned into a base, then each mesh of the second operand is subtracted from it: meshDifference(a, b) subtracts b, meshDifference(a, [b, c]) subtracts both b and c, and meshDifference([a, b], c) subtracts c from the union of a and b. SDF operands get fidget mesh-out before Manifold DIFFERENCE composes. The result is a mesh, not an SDF: do not pass meshDifference()'s result into min/max/arithmetic. For pure-SDF math, use max(a, -b) instead. The plan slicer collapses contiguous SDF subtrees so meshDifference(sphere, box) costs the same as max(sphere, -box), but every meshDifference that touches a mesh() literal forces at least one fidget mesh-out per pure-SDF subtree it encloses. The render preview lowers meshDifference to max(a, -b) for visualization; the actual mesh-     out + Manifold composition only runs when the user clicks Save STL.",

	// Topology-reference operations (folded to constants server-side)

	"facePoint":          "Resolves the point on face 'face' of an annotated (STEP-imported) body at surface parameter (u, v): facePoint(body, face, [u, v]). Returns a constant world-frame [x, y, z], placed by the body's mesh(...) matrix. All arguments must be constants; the call is folded to a constant before compilation, so it composes anywhere a constant vector is legal. References are typically inserted by the editor's point-select tool and re-resolve against the body on every compile. An optional trailing transform (16 column-major values, e.g. the xf a branch returns or a matrix-package product) REPLACES the mesh(...) placement: pass it when the same mesh id is instanced by more than one mesh() declaration, where the implicit placement lookup is ambiguous.",
	"facePointNormal":    "Like facePoint, but returns two values — the point and the face's outward unit normal at it: pt, n = facePointNormal(body, face, [u, v]). Both are constant world-frame vectors. Because it returns two values, it may only appear as the right-hand side of a two-name assignment or as an element of a return list — not inside an expression (assign first, then use the names). Accepts the same optional trailing transform as facePoint.",
	"edgePoint":          "Resolves the point on edge 'edge' of an annotated (STEP-imported) body at curve parameter t: edgePoint(body, edge, t), or edgePoint(body, edge, component, t) for a split edge. Returns a constant world-frame [x, y, z], placed by the body's mesh(...) matrix. All arguments must be constants; the call is folded to a constant before compilation. Accepts the same optional trailing transform as facePoint.",
	"edgePointNormal":    "Like edgePoint, but returns two values — the point and an outward unit normal at it: pt, n = edgePointNormal(body, edge, t) (or with a split-edge component). Both are constant world-frame vectors. Because it returns two values, it may only appear as the right-hand side of a two-name assignment or as an element of a return list. Accepts the same optional trailing transform as facePoint.",
	"vertexPoint":        "Resolves the position of vertex 'vertex' of an annotated (STEP-imported) body: vertexPoint(body, vertex). Returns a constant world-frame [x, y, z], placed by the body's mesh(...) matrix. All arguments must be constants; the call is folded to a constant before compilation. Accepts the same optional trailing transform as facePoint.",
	"vertexPointNormal":  "Like vertexPoint, but returns two values — the position and an outward unit normal: pt, n = vertexPointNormal(body, vertex). Both are constant world-frame vectors. Because it returns two values, it may only appear as the right-hand side of a two-name assignment or as an element of a return list. Accepts the same optional trailing transform as facePoint.",
	"circleCenter":       "Resolves the center of circular edge 'edge' of an annotated (STEP-imported) body: circleCenter(body, edge). Returns a constant world-frame [x, y, z], placed by the body's mesh(...) matrix. All arguments must be constants; the call is folded to a constant before compilation. Accepts the same optional trailing transform as facePoint.",
	"circleCenterNormal": "Like circleCenter, but returns two values — the center and the circle's axis as a unit vector: c, axis = circleCenterNormal(body, edge). Both are constant world-frame vectors. Because it returns two values, it may only appear as the right-hand side of a two-name assignment or as an element of a return list. Accepts the same optional trailing transform as facePoint.",

	// Mesh-property queries (folded to constants server-side)

	"meshVolume":         "The enclosed volume of an imported mesh (STL or STEP), in cubic millimeters: meshVolume(id). `id` is the same mesh id used by mesh(id) — e.g. the value of an importStep/importMesh expression. The argument must be a constant; the call is folded to a constant before compilation, so it composes anywhere a constant number is legal. Placements are rigid, so the volume is placement-independent.",
	"meshArea":           "The total surface area of an imported mesh (STL or STEP), in square millimeters: meshArea(id). `id` is the same mesh id used by mesh(id) — e.g. the value of an importStep/importMesh expression. The argument must be a constant; the call is folded to a constant before compilation, so it composes anywhere a constant number is legal (e.g. a modulus estimate meshVolume(id) / meshArea(id) for ordering parts by size). Placements are rigid, so the area is placement-independent.",
	"meshMaxThickness":   "The maximum medial-axis wall thickness of a STEP-imported body, in millimeters: meshMaxThickness(id). This is the diameter of the largest inscribed (medial) ball anywhere in the part — the heaviest section of the part, typically found at wall junctions and bosses. `id` is the same mesh id used by mesh(id). The argument must be a constant; the call is folded to a constant before compilation, so it composes anywhere a constant number is legal (e.g. sizing a boss from the heaviest section). Requires a STEP import whose conversion computed the medialThickness annotation.",
	"meshThickestPoint":  "The location of the maximum medial-axis wall thickness of a STEP-imported body: meshThickestPoint(id [, transform]). Returns a constant [x, y, z] at the surface point whose inscribed medial ball is largest — the part's heaviest section. Useful for positioning features at the thickest wall. Placement follows the facePoint rule: the body's single mesh() placement applies implicitly; when the id is instanced by several mesh() declarations that is ambiguous and the call errors — pass `transform` (16 column-major values, e.g. the xf a branch returns), which replaces the implicit placement. `id` is the same mesh id used by mesh(id). All arguments must be constants; the call is folded to a constant before compilation. Requires a STEP import whose conversion computed the medialThickness annotation.",
	"meshThickestPoints": "Distinct heaviest sections of a STEP-imported body: meshThickestPoints(id [, transform] [, count]) for all surfaces, or meshThickestPoints(id [, transform], axis, maxAngleDeg [, count]) to keep only surfaces facing away from a direction — a point qualifies when dot(normal, normalize(axis)) < -cos(maxAngleDeg), its outward normal within maxAngleDeg of -axis, e.g. surfaces facing back toward a neighbouring part when `axis` is the part's outward x direction. Returns a constant list of [x, y, z] points, thickest first, up to `count` (default 4): after a spot is taken, qualifying points within that spot's medial-ball diameter count as the same section and are skipped. Placement follows the facePoint rule: the body's single mesh() placement applies implicitly (points placed, normals rotated for the axis test); when the id is instanced by several mesh() declarations that is ambiguous and the call errors — pass `transform` (16 column-major values, e.g. the xf a branch returns), which replaces the implicit placement. `id` is the same mesh id used by mesh(id); all arguments must be constants (imported-package constants like dirX work) and the call folds to a constant list before compilation (index it, or map over it, to place features). Errors when no surface point passes the filter. Requires a STEP import whose conversion computed the medialThickness annotation.",
}
